  ## Critical findings

  ### 1. Selected users cannot complete the product’s central journey

  The invitation and portrait screens exist, but no production component links or redirects to them. After acceptance, the invitation screen sends the person back to /, not into portrait
  creation (src/app/(selection)/invitation.tsx:119). The only global journey guard handles missing profiles, not invitations or accepted selections (src/components/shared/
  OnboardingGate.tsx:28).

  Consequences:

  - A selected user opening their push notification lands on Today.
  - They are not shown their pending invitation.
  - After accepting, they are not guided to write their portrait.
  - There is no “submitted / under review / approved / live” status surface.
  - There is no way to resume a draft from normal navigation.

  This alone prevents the actual product loop from completing.

  ### 2. Today’s Human cannot answer the world

  The database has answer and answered_at columns (supabase/migrations/20260822120000_today.sql:37), and the UI can display an answer, but there is:

  - no answer_question RPC;
  - no authorized database update path;
  - no answering screen;
  - no selected-user question inbox;
  - no skip/answer controls.

  The audience can ask and vote, but the core pipeline stops before “Today’s Human answers,” contrary to the constitution’s question pipeline (docs/PRODUCT_CONSTITUTION.md:483).

  The “answered” notification is therefore currently unreachable in normal operation.

  ### 3. Backup notification timing is broken

  Invitations expire after 12 hours (supabase/migrations/20260822060000_invitations.sql:92), but the notification sender runs only once daily at 00:15 UTC (supabase/
  migrations/20260823120000_orchestration.sql:149).

  That means:

  - the primary receives a timely push;
  - a backup promoted after a decline may wait until the next day;
  - a backup promoted after expiration will generally reach their deadline before the next notification run.

  The simulation confirms that a backup gets a database invitation, not that the backup receives an actual push.

  There is also no transactional email fallback, so users who never manually enabled push may never know they were selected.

  ### 4. Cycle failure handling is incomplete

  The constitution requires an unfilled cycle to become cancelled at 22:00 UTC on D-1 (docs/PRODUCT_CONSTITUTION.md:283). No scheduled cutoff operation exists.

  Additionally:

  - portrait rejection sets replacement_required, but nothing automatically escalates and notifies the next candidate;
  - account suspension/deletion does not consistently repair an active selection;
  - late backup invitations can continue after the cycle is no longer fillable;
  - draw and publication cron execution is not recorded with the same outcome monitoring used for Edge Functions.

  The operations log labels an HTTP job “ok” when it was merely queued, before its response is known (supabase/migrations/20260823120000_orchestration.sql:42).

  ### 5. Moderation cannot currently fulfil its safety promise

  The portrait moderation queue returns a photo path and metadata, but not the portrait answers (supabase/migrations/20260822140000_moderation.sql:638). The admin interface displays only
  the person’s name, country, verification level, and report count before approving (src/app/admin/index.tsx:102).

  Moderators therefore cannot properly inspect:

  - the photograph;
  - the seven portrait responses;
  - the content targeted by a report.

  Worse, resolving a report as “actioned” only changes the report status. It does not hide the reported question, unpublish a portrait, or suspend its author (supabase/
  migrations/20260823060000_fix_moderation_casts.sql:145).

  Other safety gaps:

  - Questions have no report action.
  - Archived Humans have no report action.
  - The production Today screen reports only the portrait.
  - block_user exists, but onBlock is never provided outside developer previews (src/components/shared/ReportAction.tsx:30).
  - There is no Blocked Users management screen.
  - The promised appeal/re-review workflow is absent.
  - Independent Archive-removal requests are absent.

  This conflicts with the App Store documentation claiming reporting and blocking are implemented (docs/APP_STORE.md:43).

  ### 6. Privacy implementation has material gaps

  The constitution explicitly says account deletion deletes media (docs/PRODUCT_CONSTITUTION.md:400). The delete function removes files from avatars, but does not remove the selected
  person’s files from the portraits bucket (supabase/functions/delete-account/index.ts:81).

  Those files become inaccessible through normal policies, but remain stored.

  The data export also omits several categories, including:

  - portrait and portrait elements;
  - vote history;
  - notification settings, tokens, and log;
  - analytics associated with the user;
  - relevant moderation/account records.

  It then copies the entire export to the system clipboard (src/app/settings/privacy.tsx:38), which is not an ideal default for sensitive personal data.

  ### 7. Account switching can expose stale private state

  Authentication state is not centralized. Every call to useSession() creates its own Supabase subscription (src/features/auth/useSession.ts:21), producing multiple independent listeners
  and temporary inconsistencies.

  Several private query keys are not user-scoped:

  - moderator identity and moderation queues (src/features/moderation/hooks.ts:25);
  - Founding Human status (src/features/profiles/hooks.ts:73);
  - notification settings.

  On a shared device, a moderator signing out and another account signing in can temporarily inherit cached moderator state and cached queue content. Database permissions still prevent
  unauthorized writes, but private data may remain visible in memory.

  Questions are also persisted as “public” offline data (src/lib/offline/persist.ts:28), even though their result includes personalized has_voted state and applies the current user’s
  block list (supabase/migrations/20260822150000_privacy.sql:150).

  Push tokens are never unregistered on sign-out, so the device can continue receiving the former account’s selection or answer notifications (src/features/auth/api.ts:91).

  ### 8. UTC rollover can show the wrong Human

  The Today query has a five-minute stale time but no React Native AppState/focus integration or scheduled invalidation at the UTC boundary (src/features/daily-human/hooks.ts:20).

  The timer recalculates against the new UTC date, while the cached Human remains unchanged. An app left open across midnight can therefore show yesterday’s Human with nearly 24 hours
  remaining.

  This directly undermines “one global clock.”

  ## Important product and retention gaps

  ### Remember has no memory

  The constitution defines Remember as adding someone to a private “Humans I remember” library (docs/PRODUCT_CONSTITUTION.md:500). The database stores the relationship, but no library
  route, query, or profile section exists.

  This is the clearest concept-aligned retention opportunity: people should be able to return to stories they intentionally kept.

  ### The Archive stops after 30 people

  The API supports offset pagination, but the hook always requests the first page and the screen has no “load more” behavior (src/features/archive/api.ts:23, src/features/archive/
  hooks.ts:28).

  The Archive therefore becomes less complete as the product succeeds.

  It also lacks explicit:

  - Yesterday;
  - Humans I remember;
  - localised dates;
  - deep links to a stable archived web page.

  Archived sharing generates /human/{drawId} (src/constants/links.ts:31), but that route is not among the website’s generated pages (website/src/content/site.ts:3). Recipients without
  the installed app will receive a 404.

  ### Translation is invisible

  getPortraitTranslations() exists (src/features/daily-human/api.ts:151), and HumanPortrait supports translated content, but Today and Archive never fetch or pass translations.

  Profile locale also defaults to English in the database and is never synchronized when the user changes app language. Consequently, server notifications can remain English for French
  or German users.

  The notification copy is independently hard-coded inside the Edge Function rather than derived from the app’s translation catalogue (supabase/functions/send-notifications/index.ts:40).

  ### Notification categories are overstated

  Of the four advertised categories:

  - Daily: conditionally functional.
  - Selected: primary only, provided the user manually registered push.
  - Answered: impossible until answering exists.
  - Anniversary: declared, but absent from notifications_due().

  There is also no notification response handler, routing, foreground handler, actionable Accept/Decline notification, or notification_opened analytics implementation. The notification
  library entry is currently empty (src/lib/notifications/index.ts:1).

  ### Retention analytics need correction before trusting the growth gate

  Examples:

  - today_viewed fires when the screen mounts, including loading errors and Quiet Days, rather than after a Human is successfully displayed (src/app/(tabs)/index.tsx:65).
  - human_remembered fires for both remembering and forgetting (src/app/(tabs)/index.tsx:213).
  - question_voted fires for votes and unvotes.
  - app_opened fires only when the root mounts, not once per active UTC day when an existing process resumes.
  - notification_opened is never recorded.
  - Image sharing counts opening the system share sheet as completion.

  The retention infrastructure is thoughtful, but its inputs are not yet reliable enough for growth decisions.

  ### Profile and enrollment friction

  Onboarding currently asks users to type:

  - a two-character country code;
  - a unique social-style username.

  Languages are omitted during onboarding and later edited as comma-separated codes. Country validation accepts any two letters rather than a real ISO country.

  wants_selection defaults to true (supabase/migrations/20260822020000_selection.sql:25), while the user is never explicitly asked whether they want to join the draw. Community rules
  must then be discovered manually under Settings before eligibility becomes possible.

  A country picker, language picker, explicit selection choice, and direct rules CTA would make enrollment clearer and more trustworthy.

  ## Concept-safe retention direction

  Unumae should not solve retention with feeds, streaks, rankings, artificial urgency, or generic re-engagement messages.

  The strongest return mechanisms already belong naturally to the concept:

  - a genuinely new Human at the UTC boundary;
  - notifications when your question is answered;
  - the private Remember library;
  - Yesterday as a second chance;
  - meaningful anniversaries from your remembered Humans;
  - visible progress when you are selected;
  - knowing that one day the selected person could be you;
  - share links that let non-users actually read the story.

  These deepen memory and human connection without turning people into engagement inventory.

  ## Recommended implementation plan

  ### Phase 0 — Correct the core loop before beta

  1. [x] Create one user-journey coordinator that returns pending invitation, accepted selection, portrait status, live status, and required next action.
  2. [x] Surface pending selection globally and route notification opens to the invitation.
  3. [x] Route acceptance directly to the portrait builder and support draft resumption.
  4. [x] Add a selected-person status screen for draft, review, approval, rejection, live, and archived.
  5. [x] Implement answer_question with strict selected-user, approved-question, live-cycle, and length checks.
  6. [x] Build the “Answer the world” screen with top questions, optional answering, and no pressure to answer everything.
  7. [x] Add UTC-boundary and AppState refresh behavior.
  8. [x] Add end-to-end tests that operate through the same application APIs and routes users use.

  Exit criterion: a newly selected user can receive an alert, accept, create a portrait, be moderated, go live, answer a question, and enter the Archive without manual URLs or database
  intervention.

